## A render-free core

The surface rests on the simulation being steppable apart from its drawing.
`specs/simulation.md` fixes the game's timestep at `TICK_HZ` (`120`) and its
per-tick order, so a step means the same thing whatever the frame rate, and the
game's state advances from the elapsed time it is handed, independent of a canvas,
of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock time. The dependency runs
one way: the simulation reads nothing from the renderer.

Where a rule draws at random, the surface poses the outcome the draw decides
before the game makes it, through the operations under Posed draws below. A posed
outcome is a precondition like any other pose: the rule that draws runs from it
exactly as it runs from a drawn one.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the game, and the
surface carries no operation for either. That layer reports which keys are down
and draws and toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception. The surface is what takes the game off real time and
advances it, through `setAutoStep` and `advance` below.

Muting is the other. There is no operation that sets it: mute is reached the way a
player reaches it, through its key in `specs/controls.md`, and the snapshot reports
the result.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the audio bus, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the
root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The
engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it drives
the actions the game registers, it owns muting, and it draws and toggles the debug
overlay.

There is therefore no operation that sets muting: mute is reached the way a player
reaches it, through its action in `specs/controls.md`, and the snapshot reports the
result.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Shatter's, under Diagnostics below.

## Units

Every `x`, `y`, `vx`, and `vy` the surface takes or reports is in the logical units
of the fixed `1280 x 720` field, and every position is an entity's centre. Angles
are in radians. Durations are in seconds, with one exception: `fireCooldown` is in
whole simulation ticks, because `specs/weapons.md` fixes the gun's gate in ticks.

## Identity

{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
Every rock, bullet, saucer bullet, and torpedo carries an `id`: a number, distinct
among the entities live at any moment, reported by `snapshot` and taken by every
per-entity operation.
{{else}}
Every rock, bullet, and saucer bullet carries an `id`: a number, distinct among the
entities live at any moment, reported by `snapshot` and taken by every per-entity
operation.
{{/if}}

Two rules make an id findable without an assignment scheme:

- An entity added through this surface is appended to its roster, so it is the last
  entry and its id is read from there.
- The two fragments a split produces are appended in order, each with a fresh id,
  and the destroyed rock's id is not reused while any live entity holds it.

The saucer carries an id too, reported as `saucer.id`. No operation takes it, since
there is one slot to address. It is fresh on every arrival, distinct among every
live entity, and not reused while any live entity holds it, so one visit is
distinguishable from the next.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`SHATTER_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, booleans, and the two
records the readings below return.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation takes the current state, `DeepReadonly<ShatterState>`, as its first
parameter. A pose returns the next `ShatterState`, built from the one it was
handed, which it leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read. The signatures
below name the parameters that follow the state, so `setShipPosition(x, y)` below
is `setShipPosition(state, x, y)` on the surface and `snapshot()` is
`snapshot(state)`.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}

Each operation takes only the parameters its signature names and acts on the live
game the moment it is called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it
read and changes nothing.
{{/if}}

### The core

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}| `reset()` | Restores the state to the title-screen values listed below. |
{{else}}| `reset()` | Restores every declared field of the state to its title-screen value, listed below. |
{{/if}}| `snapshot()` | A pure read of the state, returned as the object under Snapshot shape below. It changes nothing. |
| `menuItemRect(index)` | The hit region of the entry at `index` on the menu the current screen shows. It changes nothing. |

`reset` restores `screen` to `"title"`, `menuIndex` to `0`, `score` to `0`,
`lives` to `START_LIVES` (`3`), `wave` to `0`, and `waveBanner` to `0`. It empties
the rock, bullet, and saucer-bullet rosters and removes the saucer. It places the
ship at the safe point `(SAFE_X, SAFE_Y)` = `(640, 560)` at rest facing `FACE_UP`,
with `invuln` `0`, `fireCooldown` `0`, and its contact gate on. It turns the two
world gates `waveSpawning` and `saucerSpawning` back on, returns the saucer's
arrival clock to the start of a game's cadence, and sets `simTime` to `0`.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
It empties the torpedo roster and sets `torpedoCharge` to `1`.
{{/if}}

It clears every posed draw, returning `nextSaucerEdge`, `nextSaucerRow`,
`nextSaucerAim`, `nextRockSpeed`, and `nextRecycleEdge` to `null`. `muted` is left
exactly as it stands; muting is the runtime's.

`menuItemRect` returns `{ x, y, w, h }` in logical units, with `x` and `y` the
region's top-left corner and `w` and `h` its size: the region a pointer selects
that entry from, as `specs/ui.md` states. It returns `null` on `howto` and
`playing`, which show no menu, and when `index` names no entry of the menu the
current screen shows. It is a pure read, and where the regions are is the build's
own layout rather than anything this specification fixes.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}

### The clock

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setAutoStep(enabled)` | Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. |
| `advance(ticks)` | Runs whole simulation ticks immediately. |

`setAutoStep(false)` stops the frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall
clock, so the game changes only when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns
it to running itself, which is how the game starts. Drawing is unaffected either
way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows the state the
most recent tick left. It changes no game state. `reset` leaves this setting
exactly as it stands: the clock is the runtime's rather than a game value, so a
reset taken under a held clock leaves the clock held.

`advance(ticks)` runs `ticks` whole simulation ticks immediately and in order, each
worth exactly one `TICK_DT`. `ticks` is a non-negative whole number, and
`advance(0)` runs nothing at all. Each is a real tick, the same one the frame loop
runs, followed by a render, so the game's own rules produce the result and the
canvas reflects it.
{{/if}}

### The screen and the run

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setScreen(screen)` | Sets the current screen: `"title"`, `"howto"`, `"playing"`, `"paused"`, or `"gameover"`. It spawns nothing and clears nothing. |
| `setMenuIndex(n)` | Sets the highlighted entry of whatever menu the current screen shows, counted from `0`. |
| `setScore(n)` | Sets the score. |
| `setLives(n)` | Sets the ships the player has left, counting the one in play. |
| `setWave(n)` | Sets the current wave number. It spawns no rocks and clears none. |
| `setWaveBanner(seconds)` | Sets the seconds left on the `WAVE N` banner; `0` clears it. |

`setScore` grants no extra ship, whatever multiple of `EXTRA_LIFE_STEP` it carries
the score across.

`setWaveBanner` sets the timer and nothing else. The banner is the whole of the
arming: the game remembers no pending wave beside it, so a posed banner runs down
and puts up the wave its number names exactly as one a clear raised does
(`specs/progression.md`), and posing `0` takes the banner and the wave behind it
away together. Spawning stays under `setWaveSpawning` below, so a posed banner over
a shut gate runs out and puts up nothing.

### The world gates

Two faculties belong to the game itself rather than to any one entity. Each has its
own gate, each gates one faculty and nothing else, each is on by default and
restored to on by `reset`, and each is reported by `snapshot`.

| Operation | Gates |
| --- | --- |
| `setWaveSpawning(enabled)` | The game's own wave loop: noticing that the last rock has been destroyed, raising the `WAVE N` banner, advancing the wave number, and spawning the wave the banner announces. Off, an emptied field stays empty and the wave number holds. A banner already running still runs down. |
| `setSaucerSpawning(enabled)` | The game's own arrival of a saucer, at `SAUCER_FIRST_DELAY` and at the gaps after it. Off, no saucer appears unless one is added. A saucer already on the field carries on and still leaves on its own clock. |

### The saucer cadence

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setSaucerDue(seconds)` | Sets `saucerDue`, the seconds `saucerClock` must reach for the next saucer to arrive, which is the figure the gap draw decides. It moves nothing else: `saucerClock` stands where it is. |

A saucer's departure draws a fresh `saucerDue` as `specs/saucer.md` states, so a
value posed while a saucer is up is replaced when that saucer leaves, and a value
posed while none is up stands until the arrival it times.

The cadence runs whenever the field holds no saucer. A saucer taken off through
`removeSaucer()` is followed by the next arrival once `saucerClock` reaches
`saucerDue`, exactly as one that left on its own clock is.

### The ship

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setShipPosition(x, y)` | Places the ship's centre at a logical field position. |
| `setShipVelocity(vx, vy)` | Sets the ship's velocity, in logical units per second. |
| `setShipAngle(radians)` | Sets the ship's facing. It changes no velocity. |
| `setShipInvuln(seconds)` | Sets the seconds of respawn grace remaining; `0` clears it, so lethal contact resumes. It sets the timer alone, and whether a contact then destroys the ship is decided by the game's own collision rules. |
| `setFireCooldown(ticks)` | Sets the whole ticks until the gun may fire again. |
| `setShipCollision(enabled)` | Gates the ship's lethal contact test, which is whether a rock, the saucer, or a saucer bullet reaching it destroys it and costs a life, and nothing else. Off, the ship still flies, still turns, still fires, still slides along the star's core, and no contact costs anything. |

`setShipCollision` leaves the ship-and-core rule alone: the slide is a separate,
non-lethal interaction and runs whether the gate is on or off.

### The bullets

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `addBullet(x, y, vx, vy)` | Adds one of the ship's bullets in flight, its centre at a logical field position, with a full `BULLET_LIFE`. It is appended to `bullets` and takes a fresh id, and it then obeys the well, wraps, and resolves its hits through the game's own rules. |
| `removeBullet(id)` | Removes the bullet with that id. |
| `clearBullets()` | Removes every one of the ship's bullets, leaving the rocks, saucer bullets, and the saucer standing. |
| `addEnemyBullet(x, y, vx, vy)` | Adds one saucer bullet in flight, with a full `SAUCER_BULLET_LIFE`. Appended, fresh id, live in the same way. |
| `removeEnemyBullet(id)` | Removes the saucer bullet with that id. |
| `clearEnemyBullets()` | Removes every saucer bullet, leaving the rest standing. |

There is no operation that fires. A caller that wants a round places one; a caller
checking firing itself drives the fire key and reads the bullets that appear.

### The rocks

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}| `addRock(size, x, y)` | Adds one rock of `size` (`"large"`, `"medium"`, or `"small"`) with its centre at a logical field position, at rest, at full health for its size, with the collision radius its size fixes. It is appended to `rocks` and takes a fresh id. |
{{else}}| `addRock(size, x, y)` | Adds one rock of `size` (`"large"`, `"medium"`, or `"small"`) with its centre at a logical field position, at rest, with the collision radius its size fixes. It is appended to `rocks` and takes a fresh id. |
{{/if}}
| `setRockVelocity(id, vx, vy)` | Sets that rock's velocity, in logical units per second. |
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
| `setRockHealth(id, hp)` | Sets that rock's remaining hits, a whole number from `1` to the full health of its size. |
{{/if}}
| `removeRock(id)` | Removes the rock with that id. |
| `clearRocks()` | Removes every rock, leaving the bullets, saucer bullets, and the saucer standing. It destroys nothing and scores nothing, so a field it emptied has had no rock destroyed on that tick. |

### The saucer

At most one saucer exists at a time, so the saucer is a single slot rather than a
roster: `snapshot().saucer` is the address, and `removeSaucer()` is both the
per-entity removal and the clear.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `addSaucer(x, y)` | Brings a saucer onto the field with its centre at a logical field position, travelling right at `SAUCER_SPEED` with no vertical component, its weave direction `1`, its fire clock at `SAUCER_FIRE_INTERVAL`, its weave clock at `SAUCER_WEAVE_INTERVAL`, its own lifetime clock at zero, and all three faculties on. It replaces any saucer already up. |
| `setSaucerVelocity(vx, vy)` | Sets the saucer's velocity, in logical units per second. |
| `setSaucerWeave(direction)` | Sets the saucer's weave direction, `1` for down or `-1` for up: the direction its next reroll takes while it has no vertical velocity, as `specs/saucer.md` states. It changes no velocity. |
| `removeSaucer()` | Clears the saucer from the field. |
| `setSaucerMind(enabled)` | Gates the saucer's steering decisions alone: the vertical weave it rerolls every `SAUCER_WEAVE_INTERVAL` and the steering that keeps it clear of the star's core. Off, nothing it decides changes its velocity; it still travels and still fires. |
| `setSaucerGun(enabled)` | Gates the saucer's firing alone: the aimed shot it takes every `SAUCER_FIRE_INTERVAL`. Off, it fires nothing; it still steers and still travels. |
| `setSaucerTravel(enabled)` | Gates the saucer's locomotion alone: its centre holds where it stands. Its mind and its gun run on, so a held saucer still rerolls its weave and still fires. |

### Posed draws

Each operation below sets the outcome the game's next draw of one kind would
decide, in place of the draw `specs/simulation.md` lists. A pose stands until the
draw it names is made, which consumes it and returns its field to `null`; a call
before then replaces it, and every draw after a consumed pose is random again. The
field a pose is reported under is named in its row.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setNextSaucerEdge(edge)` | Poses the edge, `"left"` or `"right"`, the next saucer the game's own cadence brings in enters at. Reported as `nextSaucerEdge`. |
| `setNextSaucerRow(y)` | Poses the row, in logical units, the next saucer the game's own cadence brings in enters at. Reported as `nextSaucerRow`. |
| `setNextSaucerAim(radians)` | Poses the aim error of the next shot the saucer fires, in radians, in place of the draw from `-SAUCER_AIM_ERROR` to `+SAUCER_AIM_ERROR`. Reported as `nextSaucerAim`. |
| `setNextRockSpeed(speed)` | Poses the base drift speed, in logical units per second, that every rock of the next placement takes: the rocks of the next wave the game spawns, or the next rock the star recycles, whichever comes first. A wave's multiplier still applies over it. Reported as `nextRockSpeed`. |
| `setNextRecycleEdge(edge)` | Poses the edge, `"top"`, `"bottom"`, `"left"`, or `"right"`, the next rock the star recycles re-enters at. The point on that edge is still drawn. Reported as `nextRecycleEdge`. |

An arrival consumes whichever of `nextSaucerEdge` and `nextSaucerRow` stands and
draws the other. A saucer `addSaucer` brings on makes no entry draw, so neither
pose is consumed by it, and a shot `addEnemyBullet` places makes no aim draw. A
rock `addRock` places is at rest and consumes no `nextRockSpeed`.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}

### The torpedoes

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setTorpedoCharge(fraction)` | Sets the stored charge, a number from `0` to `1`. |
| `addTorpedo(x, y, heading)` | Adds one torpedo in flight, its centre at a logical field position, travelling at `TORPEDO_SPEED` along `heading` in radians, with a full `TORPEDO_LIFE` and its guidance on. Appended to `torpedoes`, fresh id. |
| `setTorpedoHeading(id, radians)` | Sets that torpedo's heading. Its speed is unchanged, so its velocity turns with it. |
| `setTorpedoHoming(id, enabled)` | Gates that torpedo's guidance alone: the forward-cone acquisition and the turn onto a target. Off, it holds its heading. Its travel, its lifetime, and its impacts run on. |
| `removeTorpedo(id)` | Removes the torpedo with that id. |
| `clearTorpedoes()` | Removes every torpedo, leaving the rest standing. |
{{/if}}

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot` returns exactly this object. Every field an operation can set is
present, so every operation is verifiable by setting a value and reading it back.
The two exceptions are `tickClock` and `nextId`, which `reset` restores and
nothing else touches: they are the game's own bookkeeping, and what they do is
already observable through the ids fresh entities take and the ticks a frame runs.

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "paused" | "gameover",
  menuIndex: <number>,          // the highlighted entry, from 0
  score: <number>,
  lives: <number>,              // ships left, INCLUDING the one in play
  wave: <number>,               // 0 before the first wave
  waveBanner: <number>,         // seconds left on the WAVE N banner, 0 when none
  muted: <boolean>,
  waveSpawning: <boolean>,      // the game's own wave loop runs
  saucerSpawning: <boolean>,    // the game's own saucer arrival runs
  saucerClock: <number>,        // seconds the current arrival cadence has run
  saucerDue: <number>,          // seconds saucerClock must reach for the next one
  nextSaucerEdge: "left" | "right" | null,   // the posed entry edge; null when none stands
  nextSaucerRow: <number> | null,            // the posed entry row
  nextSaucerAim: <number> | null,            // the posed aim error of the next shot
  nextRockSpeed: <number> | null,            // the posed base speed of the next placement
  nextRecycleEdge: "top" | "bottom" | "left" | "right" | null,   // the posed re-entry edge
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
  autoStep: <boolean>,          // the frame loop advances the simulation
{{/if}}
  ship: {
    x: <number>, y: <number>,   // the ship's CENTRE
    vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
    angle: <number>,            // facing, in radians
    speed: <number>,            // the magnitude of the velocity
    thrusting: <boolean>,       // thrust is being applied this tick
    invuln: <number>,           // seconds of respawn grace left, 0 for none
    collision: <boolean>,       // the ship's lethal contact test runs
    fireCooldown: <number>,     // whole ticks until the gun may fire again
  },
  // Every one of the ship's bullets in flight, in roster order.
  bullets: [
    { id: <number>, x: <number>, y: <number>,
      vx: <number>, vy: <number>, life: <number> },   // life in seconds
  ],
  // Every rock on the field, in roster order.
  rocks: [
    { id: <number>, x: <number>, y: <number>,
      vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
      size: "large" | "medium" | "small",
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
      radius: <number>,
      health: <number> },       // the hits it has left
{{else}}
      radius: <number> },
{{/if}}
  ],
  // The saucer, or null when none is up.
  saucer: {
    id: <number>,               // fresh on each arrival; never reused while live
    x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
    mind: <boolean>, gun: <boolean>, travel: <boolean>,
    weave: 1 | -1,              // the direction its next reroll takes from rest
    fireClock: <number>,        // seconds until its next shot
    weaveClock: <number>,       // seconds until it rerolls its weave
    age: <number>,              // seconds it has been on the field
  } | null,
  // Every saucer bullet in flight, in roster order.
  enemyBullets: [
    { id: <number>, x: <number>, y: <number>,
      vx: <number>, vy: <number>, life: <number> },
  ],
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
  // Every torpedo in flight, in roster order.
  torpedoes: [
    { id: <number>, x: <number>, y: <number>,
      vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
      heading: <number>,        // radians
      life: <number>,
      homing: <boolean> },
  ],
  torpedoCharge: <number>,      // 0 to 1
  torpedoReady: <boolean>,      // true exactly when the charge is 1
{{/if}}
  simTime: <number>,            // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

Every field is read straight off the game's state, so what the snapshot reports is
what the game holds. Some entries need a note:

- Every `(x, y)` the surface reports or accepts is an entity's centre.
- `lives` counts every ship left including the one being flown, so a fresh game
  reports `3`. The HUD draws one glyph per ship in reserve, which is `lives - 1`.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
- `ship.speed`, `rocks[].radius`, and `torpedoReady` are built at the call from the
  fields beside them. No operation sets any of the three.
{{else}}
- `ship.speed` and `rocks[].radius` are built at the call from the fields beside
  them. No operation sets either.
{{/if}}
- `muted` is the game's copy of the runtime's mute bit, refreshed in every update.
  No operation sets it; the mute key does.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- `autoStep` is the clock's own setting rather than a value the game holds, so it
  is reported here and `reset` leaves it as it is.
{{/if}}
- `waveSpawning`, `saucerSpawning`, `ship.collision`, and the saucer's `mind`,
  `gun`, and `travel` are the gates and faculties, reported so each operation is
  verifiable by setting a value and reading it back.
- The saucer's `weave` is its weave direction, `1` or `-1`: drawn when the game
  brings it in, `1` for a saucer `addSaucer` brings in, and posed by
  `setSaucerWeave`.
- `nextSaucerEdge`, `nextSaucerRow`, `nextSaucerAim`, `nextRockSpeed`, and
  `nextRecycleEdge` are the posed draws, each `null` when no pose stands, reported
  on the same terms: a pose is read back until the draw it names consumes it.
- The saucer's `fireClock`, `weaveClock`, and `age` are the three clocks it
  carries, in seconds, and `saucerClock` and `saucerDue` are the arrival cadence's,
  reported on the same terms: `addSaucer` fixes the first three at
  `SAUCER_FIRE_INTERVAL`, `SAUCER_WEAVE_INTERVAL`, and `0`, `setSaucerDue` poses
  the last, and `reset` returns the last two to the start of a game's cadence.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
- Each torpedo's `homing` is its guidance gate, reported on the same terms.
{{/if}}
- `simTime` accumulates every tick's `TICK_DT`, whatever the screen.

## Diagnostics

The debug overlay is read-only and shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources. Register at least:

- the current `screen`, the score, the lives, and the wave;
- the ship's position, its velocity, its speed, its facing, and its remaining
  respawn grace;
- how many bullets and how many rocks are in play;
- whether a saucer is up, and its position when one is;
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
- the torpedo charge and how many torpedoes are in flight;
{{/if}}
- the accumulated simulation time.

Keep each one short enough to read on a line, and keep every source a pure read, so
watching the overlay leaves the game exactly as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, it is shown and hidden by the backtick key as `specs/controls.md` states,
it is off when the game starts, and it reads the game without changing it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Shatter's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read, as
`DeepReadonly<ShatterState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the panel,
toggling it, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Shatter's part, through
`world.diagnostics` in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function of no
arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through the world
the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing the panel and
toggling it are the engine's.
{{/if}}
